Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Strength dipped across your main lifts this week — recovery and intensity need attention before you can keep building muscle.",
  "summary": "You put in solid work this period, logging 28 sets and over 10,700 kg of total tonnage, which is a respectable output for a 28-year-old male at your training level. However, the most important signal this week is that your strength index dropped across chest, back, and legs — the three biggest muscle groups and the core of any muscle-building programme. Shoulders, biceps, and triceps held steady, which is encouraging, but the main lifts regressing tells us the body is not fully absorbing the training stimulus. Volume distribution is fairly balanced between chest and back, but legs are slightly under-represented relative to the other two, and biceps are notably low in both sets and volume share. Overall, this was a productive week in terms of effort, but the numbers are sending a clear signal to prioritise recovery and reassess loading before pushing harder.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "status": "regressing",
    "notes": "The strength index declined across the three primary movement patterns this period: chest dropped from 106 to 101, back from 104 to 100, and legs from 108 to 102. These are meaningful drops, not minor fluctuations, and they occurred simultaneously, which points to a systemic recovery issue rather than a single weak session. Shoulders, biceps, and triceps all held flat at 103, 102, and 104 respectively, which means the smaller muscle groups are at least not losing ground. However, for a muscle-building goal, the compound lifts driving chest, back, and leg development must trend upward over time. Right now they are moving in the wrong direction, and that needs to be addressed this coming period."
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
    {
      "muscleGroup": "biceps",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Biceps account for only 4% of total volume this period with just 3 sets, notably lower than triceps at 8.4% and 3 sets with much higher tonnage. For balanced arm development and elbow health, bringing biceps volume closer to triceps is worth considering."
    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "legs",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Legs represent 22.4% of volume versus chest at 26.8% and back at 28%. For a muscle-building goal, legs typically benefit from at least matching chest volume given the size of the muscle groups involved. The gap is not alarming but is worth monitoring."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": true,
  "deloadReason": "Strength indices dropped simultaneously across chest, back, and legs this period, which is a strong signal of accumulated fatigue rather than a programming issue. A short deload — reducing load by roughly 40–50% and keeping sets moderate — will allow the nervous system and muscles to recover so that progressive overload can resume next period. The previous period's note also flagged recovery as something to watch, and this period's data confirms that concern was warranted.",
  "actionItems": [
    "Treat next period as a deload: reduce the load on your main compound lifts by around 40 to 50 percent, keep the same movement patterns, and focus on quality reps and full range of motion rather than chasing numbers.",
    "Prioritise sleep and nutrition during the deload — aim for at least 7 to 9 hours of sleep per night and ensure you are eating enough protein, roughly 1.6 to 2.2 grams per kilogram of bodyweight, to support recovery and muscle retention.",
    "Add one or two sets of direct biceps work next period to close the volume gap with triceps and promote balanced arm development — even a couple of sets of curls at the end of a session will help.",
    "After the deload, gradually reintroduce load on chest, back, and leg exercises with the goal of surpassing your previous strength index peaks — track each session closely so you can catch any further regression early."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Strength index regressed simultaneously on chest, back, and legs this period despite solid total tonnage, indicating accumulated fatigue; a deload was recommended and recovery should be the priority before resuming progressive overload."
}
